Inez TODO
=========

Build a chess engine pairing MCTS/PUCT search with a chess-specific encoder
transformer (64 square tokens, geometric attention bias, source-destination
policy head, and WDL value head). Iterate rapidly on the RTX 4070 laptop and
scale training on available B200/A6000 HPC resources.

Work in order. Keep patches small and give each experiment one question to
answer. Establish correctness and evaluation before large runs.

0. Set constraints and interfaces
---------------------------------
- Architecture: MCTS/PUCT guided by a square-token encoder transformer with an
  independent rules core.
- Record target playing hardware (RTX 4070 laptop), time controls, batch sizes,
  GPU memory separately from host RAM, and HPC allocation limits/expiry.
- Choose engine language, trainer stack, inference runtime, and build system for
  both local development and Linux HPC runs.
- Keep rules, search, inference, and training separated behind versioned
  interfaces. Record provenance for external datasets and reference engines.

1. Make chess correct
---------------------
- Implement board/move types, FEN parsing, legal move generation, and
  make/unmake with side to move, castling, en passant, 50-move clock, and
  repetition history.
- Test checks, pins, double checks, promotions, castling, en passant, mate,
  stalemate, and dead positions. Distinguish claimable 50-move/threefold draws
  from automatic 75-move/fivefold draws; test checkmate precedence.
  Document any limitations of dead-position detection.
- Verify exact make/unmake state and hash restoration. Preserve full repetition
  history separately from FEN and the network's limited history window.
- Separate repetition keys from search-cache validity so history-dependent draw
  values are never reused in incompatible search paths.
- Add CI with perft, randomized differential testing against an independent
  rules engine, draw/history suites, and debug sanitizers.

  Gate: pass rules and draw-history tests before trusting training games/labels.

2. Build a playable, measurable PUCT baseline
---------------------------------------------
- Implement UCI (`position`, `go`, `stop`, `bestmove`), clock management, and
  clean shutdown with diagnostics separated from protocol output.
- Implement serial PUCT with uniform/heuristic priors and a simple leaf
  evaluator. Test traversal, value backup, and terminal scoring. Add virtual
  loss and leaf batching once serial search works; test inference with a stub.
- Add an automated match runner with paired openings, reversed colors, fixed
  opponent versions/resource limits, and crash/illegal-move/timeout tracking.
  Report uncertainty; predeclare game counts or SPRT bounds/stopping rules.
  Maintain a tactical/endgame test suite alongside matches.
- Measure nodes/sec, batch utilization, and inference latency at target time
  controls alongside fixed-node diagnostics.

  Gate: reproduce a baseline match report before evaluating trained networks.

3. Specify the transformer and data contracts
---------------------------------------------
- Input representation: 64 square tokens (oriented to side-to-move) with current
  and past piece planes (initially n=7), castling/en passant state, halfmove
  clock, and repetition features.
- Network architecture: encoder-only transformer with dynamic geometric
  attention bias (GAB), attention from-to policy head (64x64 + promotions) with
  legal-move masking, and WDL value head. Treat history depth, FFN width, and
  model size as initial experiment settings to validate, then tune.
- Data contract: versioned records storing game history, teacher evaluations,
  and move distributions. Split by game/source and audit duplicates and
  transpositions across splits; reserve an evaluation set outside routine
  tuning. Record dataset source, license, checksums, and teacher/search
  settings.
- Version model exports, feature/move encodings, and label conventions; reject
  incompatible artifacts. Validate records and terminal/teacher-score targets.
- Test exact agreement on board orientation, move indexing, legal masks, and WDL
  perspective across the rules core, trainer, and exported inference runtime.

4. Prove a small learning loop on the laptop
--------------------------------------------
- Overfit a tiny diagnostic batch on the RTX 4070 with a small transformer
  (3M-5M parameters), then train on a bounded dataset of distilled games and
  engine-annotated positions. Small HPC pilots can run alongside local work.
- Export weights, verify numerical agreement within defined precision tolerances
  between trainer and engine inference, and run controlled baseline matches.
- Save atomic checkpoints (weights, optimizer, schedule, RNG, data state, and
  git revision) and test interrupt/resume recovery.
- Save run configurations, dependency versions, dataset manifests, and hardware
  details alongside checkpoints so experiments can be reproduced.
- Play games against the user, analyze instructive positions, and fold lessons
  into the training mix.

  Gate: establish a credible playing-strength gain on the target hardware before
  committing to expensive long runs.

5. Scale on available HPC
-------------------------
- Run a bounded cluster pilot to measure data loading, GPU utilization, batch
  throughput, and storage growth.
- Add Slurm job scripts with signal handling, automatic checkpoint resume, and
  regular export of validated models outside temporary scratch storage.
- Benchmark exported checkpoints on the RTX 4070 at real time controls to ensure
  model accuracy gains outweigh inference latency.

6. Improve strength and explore original research
-------------------------------------------------
- Trial MCTS self-play once the supervised learning loop is validated; compare
  its benefit and cost with further distillation. Record generating model/search
  versions and monitor draw collapse, adjudication, and replay buffer diversity.
- Co-optimize search and inference: test FP16/INT8 quantization, batched leaf
  evaluation, and Syzygy tablebases with rule-aware probing.
- Treat graph search and new architecture ideas as optional research. Test one
  hypothesis at a time (e.g., transposition-aware graph search, action-value
  warm-starting for PUCT, distributional value targets, or early-exit layers).
  Log compute cost vs. Elo gain.

7. Develop repertoire and personality
-------------------------------------
- Experiment with curated fine-tuning data or optional skill/style conditioning
  once the strength baseline is established; measure any loss of strength.
- Add an optional machine-readable opening repertoire and measure style
  trade-offs against raw Elo.
- Track beating the user as a personal milestone alongside automated tests.

Next implementation milestone
-----------------------------
- Run the bounded 29M Elite -> Stockfish -> PUCT curriculum on Rivanna and
  verify actual CUDA execution, throughput, resume recovery, and game gates.
  The implementation and submit instructions are in docs/training.md.
- Expand the independent tactical suite and retain an untouched final test set
  before treating a checkpoint promotion as evidence of broad strength gains.
- Integrate an accepted neural artifact with the native C++ evaluator, verify
  numerical agreement, and measure paired matches at RTX 4070 time controls.
- Scale data/model size only after the pilot establishes correctness and useful
  validation/game results; the 4M diagnostic weights are not a trained player.

